<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wörlitz Park</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm is a group of landscape parks laid out in the late 18th century around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> by Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-frederick-franz">Leopold III Friedrich Franz</a> of Anhalt-Dessau and his architect Friedrich Wilhelm von Erdmannsdorff. Inspired by English gardens, it combined parks, palaces and farmland in an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> ideal of beauty and usefulness. Wörlitz Park, begun in 1769, is its centrepiece. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dessau-worlitz-garden-realm.jpg" alt="Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jwaller · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W%C3%B6rlitzAmaliengrotte.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The gardens have their roots in the seventeenth century, when Prince John George II of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> princess Henriette Catharina of Orange in 1659. She brought engineers and architects from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, who laid out a town, a palace and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> garden at Oranienbaum, and Dutch influence remained strong for decades.</p><p>Leopold III became Duke of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a> in 1758. Five years later he and his friend the architect Friedrich Wilhelm von Erdmannsdorff made a Grand Tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The ancient architecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the English landscape gardens, among them Stourhead, and the ideals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> shaped their taste. Leopold began changing the countryside in 1765, and over the next forty years he adapted several parks and palaces, including Oranienbaum, Georgium, Mosigkau and Großkühnau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Wörlitzer Park was laid out between 1769 and 1773 beside an anabranch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>. Leopold meant it to serve also as a school of architecture, gardening and agriculture, so large parts were open to the public from the start. Erdmannsdorff designed most of the buildings and Johann Friedrich Eyserbeck laid out the gardens. Wörlitz Palace, finished in 1773, was the first Neoclassical building in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The synagogue of 1790, a rotunda modelled on the Temple of Vesta at Tivoli, expressed Leopold's religious tolerance. The park also has an artificial volcano on an island, inspired by Leopold's visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and Vesuvius, and the Gothic House begun in 1774, modelled on Horace Walpole's villa at Strawberry Hill.</p><p>Other parts have their own character. Oranienbaum was rebuilt in a Chinese style in 1780, with a pagoda and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> house. Luisium, built from 1774 to 1778, was a gift to Leopold's wife Louise, and Leopold died there on 9 August 1817 after a riding accident. Mosigkau, built from 1752 to 1757, is a Rococo palace that holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flemish-baroque-painting">Flemish Baroque paintings</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The cultural landscape covers 142 square kilometres within the Middle Elbe Biosphere Reserve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>. UNESCO designated it a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2000 for its landscape design and its witness to the ideals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. The grounds were divided into four parts by a railway line and the A9 motorway. The Kulturstiftung Dessau-Wörlitz, which runs the park, has its seat in Großkühnau Castle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dessau-Wörlitz Garden Realm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His grandson Leopold III Friedrich Franz was a model Enlightenment ruler who laid out the landscape gardens of <b>Wörlitz</b>, built schools and founded the Philanthropinum, a progressive school, in 1774.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Principality of Anhalt-Dessau</a></li></ul></section>
